{
  "_note": "LIFECYCLE AND RESIDENCY — the half of this component that is not animation but MEMORY. A Rive artboard is a live scene, not a decoded image, and twenty of them in a scrolling list is the failure mode the plan named (§6). THE LAW, `schedule`. (1) A machine ADVANCES only while it is live (mounted), wanted (autoplay or an explicit play()), on screen, and the app is foreground. Every other tick is DROPPED, never queued: `time` is accumulated delta and therefore EXCLUDES the offscreen interval, exactly as the canvas frame loop does (canvas/frames.json — the one piece of U04 this component reuses wholesale). (2) UNMOUNT DISPOSES. The instance is released, `live` goes false, and a remount is a FRESH instance whose clock starts at zero and whose wanted-state returns to `autoplay` — an imperative play() does not survive the scene that received it. (3) Ticks are coalesced at the same 60/s budget the canvas uses. THE LAW, `residency`. (4) A list holds at most `capacity` live artboards, most-recently-visible first; an offscreen row is DEMOTED (it keeps its instance while there is room) and evicted before any visible one. Eviction disposes; a returning row is re-instantiated, and the corpus counts both, because 'bounded memory' is a number a fixture can assert while an allocation is not. Functions: rivePlaybackSchedule(autoplay, events) and riveResidency(capacity, events) on all three.",
